The mere fact is that the profits or even losses are distributed in accordance with the shares of the two owners does not necessarily establish a partnership within the meaning of the Partnership Act. One of the principal differences between a partnership and co- ownership is that co- ownership is not necessarily the result of agreement whereas partnership is. The co- ownership does not necessarily involve community of profit or of loss but partnership does. In a partnership each partner acts for all but a co- owner is not such an agent real or implied of the other.

Distinction between a partnership firm and co-ownership:
i) The relation of partnership arises from agreement and agreement only but co- ownership is not necessarily the result of agreement;
ii) Business is necessary in partnership whereas it is not necessary in co-ownership;
iii) There is mutual agency between partners, but one co-owner is not as such the agent of others;
iv) Sharing of profits is necessary in partnership whereas it is not necessary in co-ownership. Co-owners share only gross return;
v) A partner cannot transfer her interest to outside without the consent of all the partners, but on co-owner can;
vi) A partner cannot seek partition of the partnership property, but one co-owner always can;
vii) A partner has a lien on the partnership assets for moneys spent by him for the partnership. A co-owner has no lien under similar circumstances.

(i) Partnership is result of an agreement whereas joint family business result from status,
(ii) Death of a partner dissolves a partnership but not a joint family business;
(iii) The management of a Hindu joint family business rests with senior male members, the Karta, whereas in Partnership all partners have an equal right to participate except so far as regulated by the agreement;
(iv) A member of joint Hindu family on separation is not entitled to ask for accounts, a retiring partner is;
(v) A partnership business comes into existence by mutual contact between the partners whereas Joint Hindu Family firm is created by operation of law.
(vi) In partnership, a member is taken into the business by an agreement but in Joint Hindu Family business, one becomes the member by birth.
(vii) In partnership, all the partners are liable jointly and severally for all debts and obligation of the firm to an unlimited extent but in Joint Hindu Family business, the liability of co- partners is limited and liability of Karta (manager) is unlimited.
(viii) In partnership, implied authority vests equally in all the partners but only manager has the implied authority in Joint Hindu Family.
